## 8. How Are Annotations Used in Spring Boot?

Annotations are widely used in Spring Boot and Hibernate.

Examples include:

| Annotation | Common purpose |
|---|---|
| `@Component` | Marks a class as a Spring-managed component candidate |
| `@Service` | Indicates a service-layer component |
| `@Autowired` | Requests dependency injection |
| `@RestController` | Marks a controller that handles web requests and returns response data |
| `@Entity` | Marks a class as a JPA entity |
| `@Id` | Identifies the primary key of an entity |

For example:

```java
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
class StudentService {

    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Student service running");
    }
}
```

Spring processes annotations as part of its framework mechanisms to discover and configure application components.

Hibernate and JPA use annotations such as `@Entity` and `@Id` to describe how Java classes relate to database records.

These annotations do not work simply because Java recognizes their names; the relevant framework must process them.

---

## 9. Annotations vs Comments

| Feature | Annotation | Comment |
|---|---|---|
| Example | `@Override` | `// Override this method` |
| Meaning to tools | Can be processed by compilers or frameworks | Normally ignored by the compiler |
| Stores structured metadata | Yes | Not as Java annotation metadata |
| Can influence framework behavior | Yes, when processed | Not directly |

Comments explain code to people. Annotations provide structured metadata that tools and frameworks can interpret.

---

## 10. Important Things to Remember

- Annotations provide metadata about program elements.
- Built-in annotations include `@Override`, `@Deprecated`, and `@SuppressWarnings`.
- Developers can define custom annotations using `@interface`.
- `@Retention` controls how long annotation information is retained.
- `@Target` controls where an annotation can be applied.
- Runtime annotations can be inspected through reflection.
- An annotation alone does not implement behavior; a compiler, tool, or framework must process it.

## Key Takeaways

Annotations make Java code more descriptive and help tools and frameworks understand how classes and methods should be treated. Custom annotations let developers define their own metadata, while reflection or framework processing can turn that metadata into useful behavior.

## One-Line Summary

**Java annotations attach structured metadata to code, allowing compilers, tools, and frameworks to inspect it and act on it when configured to do so.**
